// expect_error: 18:12 'syscall' does not exist in profile 'kernel'
// expect_error_count: 1
// tests/neg/kernel_liar.fi -- ROUND 73: THE CLAIM IS CHECKED.
//
// `tests/std/kernel_liar.fi` declares `profile kernel` in its first line
// and thereby passes the import rule -- and it asks mmap for memory all
// the same. If the rule of round 73 rested on the declaration alone, this
// program would compile and a kernel would end up with an mmap in it.
//
// It does not compile. The module lands in the same compilation unit, and
// the syscall ban of the kernel profile catches it at the line where the
// `syscall` stands. THAT is what makes `profile kernel` in a library
// module a checked property instead of a promise -- and it is the reason
// the compiler needs no name list of admissible modules.
profile kernel
import std.kernel_liar

fn kmain() -> i32 {
    return kernel_liar.obtain(4096) as i32
}
